codex
The mathematical classification also supports the empty domain set. The source body is exactly `return n * n`; the nine frozen rules in `semantic.k` only register the function, bind `n`, evaluate the return expression left-to-right, perform built-in `*Int`, and store the result. None is a summary, recurrence, proof term, simplification, or domain fact, and none is in the local Stage 3 inventory. I’m confirming that operational reading with a fresh K compile and explicit runs chosen for this audit—not by executing the supplied shell script.
exec
/bin/bash -lc 'audit_k_tmp=$(mktemp -d /tmp/audit-work/stage1-operational.XXXXXX) && cp -a /reference/k-proof/. "$audit_k_tmp"/ && ( set -x; cd "$audit_k_tmp"; kompile verification.k --backend haskell --main-module VERIFICATION --syntax-module SEMANTIC-SYNTAX --output-definition verification-kompiled; krun solution.mpy --definition verification-kompiled -cN=0; krun solution.mpy --definition verification-kompiled -cN=3; krun solution.mpy --definition verification-kompiled -cN=10; krun solution.mpy --definition verification-kompiled -cN=-2; kprove spec.k --definition verification-kompiled --spec-module SPEC ) 2>&1 | tee /audit-output/evidence/18_fresh_k_operational_runs.txt' in /audit-output
